\subsection{Platform} 
We consider heterogeneous hardware platforms for edge/embedded computing, including multiple computing clusters; in particular, we focus on the Nvidia Jetson device family hosting CPU, \ac{gpu}, and \ac{dla}. 
Each cluster is provided with a \ac{dvfs} knob, with the only exception of the \ac{dla} in the platform considered in our experimental results, and the per-board power sensor.
The platform runs a standard \ac{os} as Linux, providing communication between the software modules to send and receive data and system commands. Specifically, the \ac{os} exposes interfaces for hardware control and application execution on various clusters. Platform-specific run-time frameworks are provided for executing applications on \ac{gpu} and \ac{dla}. 
% \note{the part in red is already discussed in section 2. shall we remove it? is it mandatory to say that we run an application per time on each cluster?}
% \red{\ac{gpu} and \ac{dla} support a single application to run at a single instant of time at the granularity of the single inference execution; considering each application to run a large sequence of inferences, at a coarser-grained view we see multiple applications sharing the same \ac{gpu} or \ac{dla} in time multiplexing, in a seamless way, in a similar way of a CPU core running multiple processes. Finally, \ac{dla} does not support executing all types of deep learning operators; therefore, the runtime framework automatically performs fallbacks to \ac{gpu} for unsupported operations.}
We restrict the platform's power consumption to the \ac{tdp} limit, avoiding any physical damage to the board at a high temperature. 
%We consider heterogeneous multi-core platforms supported by Linux \ac{os} enabling interfaces for workload scheduling, cluster selection, application-cluster-mapping, \ac{dvfs} control, cluster migration, and high-level application switching. The \ac{os} provides communication between the software modules to send and receive data and system commands. Twill overrides the default \ac{os} scheduling governors and enforces online actuation settings for workload deployment and execution to the platform's computational resources. The \ac{os} provides granular control over \ac{gpu} frequency enabling run-time control over \ac{dvfs}. The platform also supports run-time binding of the workload to the \ac{dla} cluster while allowing fallbacks to \ac{gpu} for unsupported operations. The platforms monitor the device's power consumption through onboard sensors or external monitoring equipment.

