\section{Mathematical Framework}

\subsection{Effective Dopamine Signaling: The Core Variable}

To move beyond qualitative descriptions, we must first establish mathematical definitions. The cornerstone of the proposed theory is effective dopamine signaling, which captures the net functional impact of dopaminergic transmission across the brain's heterogeneous architecture.

\begin{definition}[Effective Dopamine Signaling]
The effective dopamine signal $\deff(\vec{r}, t)$ at time $t$ and brain location $\vec{r}$ is defined as:
\begin{equation}
\deff(\vec{r}, t) = k(\vec{r}) \cdot \frac{\sum_{i \in \text{D1-like}} w_i(\vec{r}) \rho_i(\vec{r}) O_i(t) S_i(t)}{1 + \sum_{j \in \text{D2-like}} w_j(\vec{r}) \rho_j(\vec{r}) O_j(t) S_j(t)} \cdot \left(1 - \frac{1}{1 + \eta_{\text{DAT}}(\vec{r})^{-1}}\right)
\label{eq:deff_regional}
\end{equation}
\end{definition}

\textbf{Variable Definitions and Biological Rationale:}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{$k(\vec{r})$} = \textbf{Regional scaling factor}: Accounts for local circuit properties, baseline excitability, and anatomical connectivity. Typical values span \(0.5\) in brainstem to \(2.0\) in prefrontal cortex\cite{Marrocco2023}.

\item \textbf{$w_{*}(\vec{r})$} = \textbf{Synaptic weight}: Generic weight factor for receptor subtype $*$ (e.g.\ $i$ or $j$) at location $\vec{r}$. Captures both structural connectivity and activity-dependent plasticity.

\item \textbf{$\rho_{*}(\vec{r})$} = \textbf{Receptor density}: Number of receptors per unit volume for subtype $*$. Measured experimentally via autoradiography or PET imaging; typical range 10-500 fmol mg\(^{-1}\) protein\cite{Seeman1993,Hall2018}.

\item \textbf{$O_{*}(t)$} = \textbf{Receptor occupancy}: Fraction of subtype-$*$ receptors bound by dopamine (DA) at time $t$ ($0 \le O_* \le 1$).  
Occupancy follows Michaelis–Menten kinetics:
\[
O_{*}(t) = \frac{[\mathrm{DA}]}{K_{d,*} + [\mathrm{DA}]}
\]
where \( K_{d,*} \) is the dissociation constant for subtype $*$, representing the DA concentration at which half the receptors are occupied. Lower \( K_d \) implies higher receptor affinity.

\item \textbf{$S_{*}(t)$} = \textbf{Signaling efficiency}: Effectiveness of the receptor-G-protein cascade for subtype $*$. Accounts for desensitisation, G-protein coupling efficiency, and downstream second-messenger availability.

\item \textbf{$\eta_{\text{DAT}}(\vec{r})$} = \textbf{Dopamine transporter efficiency}: Rate of dopamine re-uptake, controlling synaptic clearance kinetics. Higher values imply faster clearance; as a rate, $\eta_{\text{DAT}}$ is non-negative, so the multiplicative term in Eq.\,(1) remains in \([0,1)\).
\end{itemize}


The ratio structure captures the fundamental opposition between D1-like receptors (which generally increase neural excitability through cAMP/PKA pathways) and D2-like receptors (which generally decrease excitability through inhibition of adenylyl cyclase) \cite{6}. This competitive interaction is central to dopamine's inverted-U dose-response curve \cite{6}.

\subsection{The Multi-State Consciousness Function}

Rather than treating consciousness as a simple threshold phenomenon, this framework recognizes it as emerging through multiple phase transitions, each governed by distinct biophysical mechanisms. In this equation, all dopamine (DA) values are expressed as a percentage of baseline unless otherwise noted.

\begin{equation}
C(\DA) = \sum_{i=1}^{n} \alpha_i \cdot \sigmoid\left(\frac{\DA - \DA_i}{\tau_i}\right) \cdot \exp\left(-\beta_i(\DA - \DA_i)^2\right) \cdot \Viability(\DA)
\label{eq:consciousness_multistate}
\end{equation}
\vspace{3cm}
\newpage
\textbf{Parameter Explanations:}
\begin{itemize}
\item $\sigmoid(x) = \frac{1}{1+e^{-x}}$ = \textbf{Sigmoid transition function}: Models the sharp but continuous transitions between consciousness states. The sigmoid captures the biological reality that state changes are neither instantaneous nor infinitely gradual.

\item $\DA_i$ = \textbf{Critical dopamine levels}: Specific thresholds for each state transition:
  \begin{align}
  \DA_1 &= 20\,\% \text{ (sleep onset)} \\
  \DA_2 &= 50\,\% \text{ (REM emergence)} \\
  \DA_3 &= 150\,\% \text{ (full wakefulness)} \\
  \DA_4 &= 250\,\% \text{ (psychosis threshold)} \\
  \DA_5 &= 320\,\% \text{ (death threshold)}
  \end{align}

\item $\tau_i$ = \textbf{Transition sharpness}: Controls how rapidly each state transition occurs. Smaller values = sharper transitions. Reflects the cooperativity of underlying neural mechanisms.

\item $\alpha_i$ = \textbf{State amplitude}: Sets the maximum contribution of each state to overall consciousness level. Reflects the "depth" or "intensity" of each consciousness mode.

\item $\beta_i$ = \textbf{Gaussian width parameter}: Prevents runaway activation at extreme dopamine values. Ensures each state has an optimal range rather than monotonic increase.

\item $\Viability(\DA)$ = \textbf{Neural viability function}: Models the lethal collapse at excessive dopamine levels (detailed below).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{The Serotonergic Complexity Modulator}

The second dimension of this consciousness space is governed by \emph{5-HT\textsubscript{2A}} receptor activation, which modulates the complexity and rule-bound nature of experience. As illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:system_comparison}, this system operates through fundamentally different mechanisms than dopamine:

\begin{equation}
\Psi_{\text{complexity}}(\HT, \DA) = \Psi_0 \left(1 + \lambda \frac{[\HT]}{K_d + [\HT]}\right) \cdot \exp\left(-\gamma\left(\frac{\DA}{\DA_{\max}}\right)^2\right) \cdot R_{\text{desens}}(t)
\label{eq:5HT_modulation}
\end{equation}

\textbf{Variable Descriptions:}

\begin{itemize}
\item $\Psi_0$ = \textbf{Baseline complexity}: The default level of cognitive complexity in the absence of \emph{5-HT\textsubscript{2A}} activation. Represents the "rule-bound" state of normal cognition.

\item $\lambda$ = \textbf{Complexity amplification factor}: Determines how strongly \emph{5-HT\textsubscript{2A}} activation increases cognitive flexibility. Typical values: $\lambda = 2.0-4.0$\cite{May1976_Logistic}. This additive formulation allows complexity to increase beyond the baseline $\Psi_0$, consistent with the profound alterations in cognition induced by \emph{5-HT\textsubscript{2A}} agonists.

\item $[\HT]$ = \textbf{\emph{5-HT\textsubscript{2A}} receptor occupancy}: Concentration of serotonin or \emph{5-HT\textsubscript{2A}} agonists bound to receptors. Unlike dopamine, this shows less regional variation.

\item $K_d$ = \textbf{Dissociation constant}: Affinity of \emph{5-HT\textsubscript{2A}} receptors for their ligands. Lower $K_d$ = higher affinity. For serotonin: $K_d \approx 10$ nM\cite{IUPHAR_5HT2A}.

\item $\gamma$ = \textbf{Dopamine interference parameter}: Models how extreme dopamine levels can interfere with \emph{5-HT\textsubscript{2A}} effects, possibly through competition for intracellular signaling resources or metabolic constraints.

\item $\DA_{\max}$ = \textbf{Maximum viable dopamine level}: The upper limit before cellular dysfunction begins ($\approx 320\,\%$ baseline).

\item $R_{\text{desens}}(t) = \exp(-t/\tau_{\text{desens}})$ = \textbf{Desensitization function}: Captures the rapid receptor desensitization that provides a critical safety valve. $\tau_{\text{desens}} \approx 2-4$ hours for \emph{5-HT\textsubscript{2A}} receptors\cite{Gray2003_5HT2A}.
\end{itemize}

The Michaelis-Menten form $\frac{[\HT]}{K_d + [\HT]}$ reflects standard receptor binding kinetics and naturally saturates at high concentrations, preventing runaway complexity increases. The desensitization term explains why psychedelic experiences are self-limiting and why tolerance develops rapidly \cite{10}.

