\section{Methods and Empirical Validation}
% +++ START: INSERT THIS NEW PARAGRAPH +++
To test the core predictions of our non-linear framework against real-world data, we operationalized its key theoretical components using the PPMI clinical dataset. Our model posits that the state of consciousness is a non-monotonic function of \textbf{Effective Dopamine Signaling (\texttt{Deff})}, which depends critically on both the availability of dopamine (\texttt{[DA]}) and the integrity of the underlying neural hardware (e.g., receptor density $\rho$, signaling efficiency $S$ in Eq.~\ref{eq:deff_regional}).

In the context of Parkinson's disease, we can map these theoretical components to clinical variables:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item We use the \textbf{Levodopa-Equivalent Daily Dose (LEDD)} as a proxy for the dopaminergic \textbf{input signal} or precursor supply, which directly influences synaptic \texttt{[DA]}.
    \item We use the \textbf{UPDRS-III motor score} as an inverse proxy for \textbf{system integrity}. Higher UPDRS scores reflect more advanced neurodegeneration, corresponding to a compromised system with lower receptor density ($\rho$), reduced signaling efficiency ($S$), and diminished neural viability.
\end{enumerate}

This mapping allows us to formulate a precise, testable hypothesis derived directly from our theory: the effect of the input signal (\texttt{LEDD}) on a consciousness-related outcome (REM sleep) should be conditional on the state of the system's integrity (\texttt{UPDRS}). Specifically, the non-linear dynamics of our model predict a significant interaction effect, where the therapeutic benefit of increasing \texttt{LEDD} diminishes or even reverses as \texttt{UPDRS} increases, reflecting a system that has moved into a dysfunctional, supra-optimal range on the dopamine-consciousness curve.
