\subsection{Statistical Analysis and Results}
\subsection{Statistical analysis}

This theory employed a hierarchical modeling approach. Model 1 tests the primary hypothesis of disease severity impact. Model 2 tests the effect of medication. Model 3, our primary model of interest, tests the theoretically-predicted interaction between disease state and medication.

To account for repeated measures, mixed-effects models were fitted with \texttt{statsmodels=0.14.1}, using participant (\texttt{patno}) as a random intercept. Based on a significant likelihood-ratio test comparing random-intercept-only vs. random-slope models ($\chi^2(2) = 45.6, p < .001$), we included random slopes for the primary predictors (UPDRS and LEDD) in all models to account for between-subject variability in these effects. The models were specified as:

\begin{align}
\text{Model 1:} &\quad \mathrm{REM}_{ij}
      = (\beta_0 + u_{0j}) + (\beta_1 + u_{1j})\,\mathrm{UPDRS}_{z,ij}
               + \beta_2\,\mathrm{Age}_{z,ij}
               + \beta_3\,\mathrm{Sex}_{ij}
               + \varepsilon_{ij} \\[4pt]
\text{Model 2:} &\quad \mathrm{REM}_{ij}
      = (\beta_0 + u_{0j}) + (\beta_1 + u_{1j})\,\mathrm{LEDD}_{z,ij}
               + \beta_2\,\mathrm{Age}_{z,ij}
               + \beta_3\,\mathrm{Sex}_{ij}
               + \varepsilon_{ij} \\[4pt]
\text{Model 3:} &\quad \mathrm{REM}_{ij}
      = (\beta_0 + u_{0j}) + (\beta_1 + u_{1j})\,\mathrm{LEDD}_{z,ij}
               + (\beta_2 + u_{2j})\,\mathrm{UPDRS}_{z,ij}
               + \beta_3\,\mathrm{LEDD}_{z,ij}\!\times\!\mathrm{UPDRS}_{z,ij} \notag \\
               & \qquad + \beta_4\,\mathrm{Age}_{z,ij}
               + \beta_5\,\mathrm{Sex}_{ij}
               + \varepsilon_{ij}
\end{align}

Continuous predictors were $z$-standardised nightly; sex is coded
$\mathrm{Male}=0,\ \mathrm{Female}=1$.
All three models converged via the Powell optimiser
(AIC\textsubscript{M1}=785123.4, AIC\textsubscript{M2}=785098.1, AIC\textsubscript{M3}=784955.6), and residual QQ-plots showed no major
deviation from normality. The lower AIC for Model 3 supports its superior fit.

