A one standard deviation increase in UPDRS score (approx. 15 points on the motor scale) is associated with a 6.5-minute reduction in nightly REM sleep. For a patient in the 75th percentile of disease severity (e.g., UPDRS-III of 40) compared to one in the 25th percentile (e.g., UPDRS-III of 10), this translates to an estimated loss of over 10 minutes of REM sleep per night, a clinically meaningful difference often associated with cognitive and affective disturbances.

