For many resolvent-based iterative methods such as the \emph{proximal point} and the \emph{forward-backward} algorithms, the fixed-point set of the underlying algorithmic operator and the set of solutions coincide. Consequently, the fixed-point set does not depend on algorithm parameters such as the stepsize. This property allows the stepsize to be varied across iterations whilst preserving an inequality in the form of \eqref{e:dr-fne} and hence the original convergence analysis holds without significant changes. In contrast, this is not the case for the DR algorithm whose fixed-point set $\Fix T_{\gamma}$ has a parametric dependence on $\gamma\in\RPP$ and need not coincide with the solution set $\zer(A+B)$. In fact, due to \cite[Proposition~26.1(i) \& Proposition~26.1(iii)(b)]{BC2017}, we have
