The last component of our analysis relies on a generalization of \emph{Fej{\'e}r monotonicity} property for sequences called the \emph{Opial property}~\cite{arakcheev2025opial}.

\begin{definition}[Opial sequence]
Let $C$ 
be a nonempty subset of $X$. A sequence $(x_n)_\nnn$  in $X$ is said to be \emph{Opial} 
with respect to 
$C$ if 
%\begin{equation*}
  $(\forall c\in C)\; \lim_{n\to\infty}\norm{x_n-c}\; $
\text{exists.}
