\section{Relocated fixed-point iterations}\label{s:abstract-framework}

%{\cite[Theorem~4.27]{BC2017}}
%---in the sense of the operator in Proposition~\ref{p:Q}---

In this section, we propose a framework for sequences constructed as fixed-point iterations of a parametrized family of operators $(T_\gamma)_{\gamma \in \Gamma}$ composed with  operators $(Q_{\delta\gets \gamma})_{\gamma,\delta\in\Gamma}$ that relocate fixed-point sets. This setting covers resolvent-based algorithms with variable stepsizes, such as the DR algorithm, as discussed by the end of this section. % in Section~\ref{sec:resolvent-splitting}. %Ultimately, this unifying framework lets us to devise, in Section~\ref{sec:resolvent-splitting}, a version of the DR algorithm that allows variable stepsizes.

We show that given a parametric family of nonexpansive operators $(T_\gamma)_{\gamma\in\Gamma}$ and a sequence of stepsizes $(\gamma_n)_\nnn$ converging to some $\overline{\gamma}$, under appropriate assumptions, the corresponding relocated fixed-point iteration generates a sequence that converges weakly to a fixed-point of $T_{\overline{\gamma}}$.% We also investigate some examples of relocated fixed-point iterations. %Ultimately, 

