\begin{remark}[On the dimension of $X$ and the parametric demiclosedness property]
\label{r:weak-conv}
    Observe that for infinite-dimensional Hilbert spaces, Assumption~\ref{a:trans}\ref{a:trans_bicont} need not imply that the operator $(x,\gamma) \mapsto T_{\gamma}x$ is (sequentially) weakly continuous on $x$ (see, e.g., \cite[page 245]{zarantonello1971projections}). Thus the parametric demiclosedness principle (Theorem~\ref{t:edemi}) is needed to obtain the results in Theorem~\ref{t:abstractconv}\ref{t:abstractconv3}.
%    
    % For instance, if $T_\gamma$ is the DR iteration operator \eqref{e:0222d}, it may not be (sequentially) weakly continuous, as the resolvent in general is not weakly continuous. An example of such a case is given in \cite[page 245]{zarantonello1971projections}. Let $X$ be the space of square summable real sequences, $A$ the normal cone of $B(0,1)$, the unit ball centered at zero, then $J_A$ is the projection onto $B(0,1)$. If $(\forall \nnn) \; e_n$ denotes the canonical sequence in $X$, that is, $(e_n)_i = 1$ if $n=i$ and $(e_n)_i = 0$ otherwise, then for $(\forall \nnn) \; x_n = e_1 + e_n $, it holds  $x_n \weak e_1$, $J_A(x_n) \weak \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}e_1$, but $J_A(e_1) \neq \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}e_1$.
 %   
    %is shown under the assumption that $X$ is a finite-dimensional Hilbert space, in order to use Assumptions~\ref{a:trans}\ref{a:trans_bicont} that states that the operator $(x,\gamma) \mapsto T_{\gamma}x$ is continuous (for the strong topology). In the infinite dimensinal case, the existence of a strong cluster point of $(x_n)_\nnn$ needs to be assumed in Theorem~\ref{t:abstractconv}\ref{t:abstractconv3}, since existence of weak cluster points instead is not enough, as the operator $(x,\gamma) \mapsto T_{\gamma}x$ may fail to be weakly continuous on $x$.
\end{remark}

