\begin{document}

\begin{abstract} We consider motion of a material point placed in a constant homogeneous magnetic field  restricted to the sphere $S^{n-1}$.
We provide a Lax representation of the equations of motion for arbitrary $n$ and prove integrability of those systems in the Liouville sense.
The integrability is provided via first integrals of degree one and two.
\end{abstract}



\maketitle


\section{Introduction. The equations of motion}




Given a material point of a unit mass in a constant homogeneous magnetic field in $\R^n$
defined by the two-form
\begin{equation}\label{polje}
\mathbf F=s\sum_{i<j}\kappa_{ij} d\gamma_i\wedge d\gamma_j,
\end{equation}
consider the motion restricted to the sphere
$
S^{n-1}=\{(\gamma_1, \dots, \gamma_n)\in\R^n\,\vert\, \langle \gamma,\gamma\rangle=\sum_{j=1}^n\gamma_j^2=1\}\subset \R^n,
$
where $\kappa =(\kappa_{ij})\in \mathrm{so}(n)$ is a fixed skew-symmetric matrix and $s\in \mathbb R\setminus \{0\}$ is a real parameter representing the minus charge.

We  consider the phase space $T^*S^{n-1}$ as a submanifold of $\mathbb{R}^{2n}(\gamma,p)$ given by the equations
$
\phi_1=\langle\gamma,\gamma\rangle=1, \, \phi_2=\langle p,\gamma\rangle=0,
$
and with the twisted symplectic form $\omega+\mathbf f$, $\omega=\Omega\vert_{T^*S^{n-1}}$, $\mathbf f=\mathbf F\vert_{T^*S^{n-1}}$. Here $\Omega$ is the standard symplectic form in $\mathbb R^{2n}$.
From now on, we use $\ell:=[n/2]$ and consider a basis $[\mathbf e_1,\dots,\mathbf e_n]$ of $\R^n$ in which the magnetic form $\mathbf F$ \eqref{polje} takes the form:
\begin{equation}\label{polje*}
\mathbf F=s(\kappa_{12} d\gamma_1\wedge d\gamma_2+\kappa_{34} d\gamma_3\wedge d\gamma_4 +\dots +\kappa_{2\ell-1,2\ell}d\gamma_{2\ell-1}\wedge d\gamma_{2\ell}),
\end{equation}
where
$\kappa_{2i-1,2i}\ge 0, \, i=1,\dots,\ell.$

The equations of a motion of material point on a unit sphere placed in the homogeneous magnetic field are
\begin{align}
\label{eq1} &{\dot\gamma}_{2i-1}=p_{2i-1},\qquad {\dot p}_{2i-1}=s\kappa_{2i-1,2i} p_{2i}+\mu\gamma_{2i-1},\\
\label{eq2} &{\dot\gamma}_{2i} =p_{2i},\qquad\qquad  {\dot p}_{2i}=-s\kappa_{2i-1,2i} p_{2i-1}+\mu\gamma_{2i}, \qquad i=1,\dots,\ell,
\end{align}
for $n$ even,
and, for $n$  odd, there is an additional couple of equations:
\begin{equation}\label{eq3}
\dot\gamma_n=p_n,\qquad\qquad {\dot p}_n=\mu\gamma_n.
\end{equation}
Here $\mu=\big({s}\langle p,\kappa\gamma \rangle-\langle p,p\rangle\big)$ is the Lagrange multiplier and $\mu \gamma$ is the reaction force of the holonomic constraint $\phi_1=1$.

These magnetic systems were obtained in \cite{DGJ2023} as a reduction of the nonholonomic problem of rolling of a ball with the gyroscope without slipping and twisting over a plane and over a sphere in $\R^n$, where the inertia operator of the system ball + gyroscope is proportional to the identity operator.   We proved integrability of the magnetic systems  on $S^2$ and $S^3$,
which correspond to  $n=3$ and $n=4$ respectively, and we performed explicit integrations of the equations of motion of these two systems in elliptic functions  in  \cite{DGJ2023}.

In our recent paper \cite{DGJ2025} we proved complete integrability of the magnetic systems on spheres $S^4$ and $S^5$, corresponding to $n=5$ and $n=6$ respectively, for any $\kappa$. The first integrals of motion for these two magnetic systems constructed there were polynomials of the  degree $1$, $2$, and $3$ in momenta. The first integrals that are polynomials linear in momenta are examples of the gauge Noether first integrals (see e.g.  \cite{CS1981}). We also  proved  in \cite{DGJ2025} noncommutative integrability of the obtained magnetic systems for any  $n\ge 7$ when a system allows a reduction to the cases with $n\le 6$, in particular, in the simplest case when
$\mathbf F=s\kappa_{12}d\gamma_1\wedge d\gamma_2$ and  for even $n$, when $\mathbf F$ is the magnetic field of the standard contact structure on $S^{n-1}$. We concluded \cite{DGJ2025} with a conjecture that restricted on $S^{n-1}$,  magnetic systems are also integrable for all $n$  and $\kappa$.

In the present note, we provide a Lax representation of the equations of motion for arbitrary $n$. We prove integrability of those systems in the Liouville sense.
The integrability is provided through  first integrals of degree one and two. Independently, such integrability has been shown by Bolsinov, Konyaev, and Matveev in \cite{BKM2025}. In \cite{DGJ2025} a parallelism of the considered magnetic systems with the classical mechanical problem of motion of a material point of mass $m$ in $\R^n$ under the influence of the potential force field with a quadratic potential
$
V(\gamma)=(a_1 \gamma_1^2+\dots+a_n\gamma_n^2)/2.
$
This system of uncoupled harmonic oscillators is trivially integrable, while the restriction of the problem to the sphere $S^{n-1}$ leads to one of the most interesting finite-dimensional  integrable systems  - the \emph{Neumann system} (see e.g. \cite{M1}). The Lax representation which we provide in Theorem \ref{th:LA} reminds of a well-known Lax representation for the Neumann system \cite{M1}. The approach of \cite{BKM2025}, though different from ours, also has the Neumann system in the background.

The Hamiltonian formalism for magnetic geodesics in a general setting was introduced in \cite{N1982}. Integrability
 of magnetic flows was studied in e.g.
 \cite{AS2020, BK2017, BJ2008, T2016, MSY2008, S2002}.

From \cite{DGJ2025}, we know that gauge Noether symmetries  are tangent to the sphere $S^{n-1}$,
and provide degree one first integrals of motion of the magnetic flows \eqref{eq1} and \eqref{eq2} for even $n$ and   \eqref{eq1},  \eqref{eq2}, and   \eqref{eq3} for odd $n$:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:fiovi}
\Phi_{2i-1,2i}=\gamma_{2i-1}p_{2i}-\gamma_{2i}p_{2i-1}+ s \frac{\kappa_{2i-1,2i}}{2}\big( \gamma_{2i-1}^2+\gamma_{2i}^2\big).
\end{equation}
In addition to the first integrals of motion $\Phi_{2i-1,2i}$, we constructed one denoted by $J$:
\begin{equation*}
J=s^2\sum_{i=1}^{\ell}\kappa_{2i-1,2i}^2 (p_{2i-1}^2+p_{2i}^2)-\mu^2, \quad
\mu= s\sum_{i=1}^{\ell}\kappa_{2i-1,2i}(p_{2i-1}\gamma_{2i}-p_{2i}\gamma_{2i-1})-2H.
\end{equation*}
All these first integrals of motion
Poisson commute with respect to the standard Poisson bracket generated by the twisted symplectic form. The functions $H$, $J$, $\Phi_{2i-1,2i}$, $i=1,\dots,\ell$ are functionally independent on $T^*S^{n-1}$ for $n\ge 5$ for all odd $n$ and all $\kappa$ and if  $n$ is even and $\kappa$ does not satisfy
$\kappa_{1,2}=\kappa_{3,4}=\dots=\kappa_{n-1,n}$. If $n$ is even and the relation  $\kappa_{1,2}=...=\kappa_{n-1,n}$ is satisfied,
then the Lagrange multiplier $\mu$ is a first integral of motion,
$
\mu=
\frac{s^2}{2}\kappa^2_{1,2}-s\kappa_{1,2}\sum_{i=1}^{n/2}\Phi_{2i-1,2i}-2H,
$
and
$
J=2s^2\kappa_{1,2}^2 H-\mu^2.
$

We will use
the equations of motion \eqref{eq1} and \eqref{eq2} rewritten in a complex notation. We set
$
z_i=\gamma_{2i-1}+\sqrt{-1}\gamma_{2i}, \, w_i=p_{2i-1}+\sqrt{-1} p_{2i}, \, i=1,\dots,\ell.
$
 Then the equations \eqref{eq1} and \eqref{eq2} take the form:
\begin{align}
\label{com1} \dot z=w, \qquad \dot w=-\sqrt{-1}Kw+\mu z,
\end{align}
where $z=(z_1,...,z_{\ell})$, $w=(w_1,...,w_{\ell})$ and $K=\diag(\kappa_{1,2},...,\kappa_{2\ell-1,2\ell})$.




\section{Lax representation and integrability for all $n$}

The cotangent bundle of the sphere $S^{2\ell-1}$ is given by 
$
T^*S^{2\ell-1}=\{(z,w)\in\mathbb C^{2\ell}\,\vert\, \langle \bar{z},z\rangle=1, \langle \bar{w},z\rangle+\langle w,\bar{z}\rangle=0\}.
$


\begin{prop} Let $R\in U(\ell)$. The action $(z,w)\mapsto (Rz,Rw)$ is a Hamiltonian action with the momentum map
$\Phi_s:T^*S^{2\ell-1}\to u(\ell)\cong u^*(\ell)$ given by
$$
\Phi_s=\frac{1}{2}(w\otimes \bar z - z\otimes \bar w)+\sqrt{-1}\frac{s}{4}(Kz\otimes \bar z +z\otimes \bar zK).
$$
Here the identification $u(\ell)\cong u^*(\ell)$ is given through an $\Ad$-invariant scalar product
on $u(\ell)$.
\end{prop}


We are going to derive some relations which are going to lead us to a Lax representation of the equations of motion.
\begin{prop}\label{pr:phis} The time derivative of the functions $\Phi_s$ satisfies the relation:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:f0}
\dot \Phi_s=\sqrt{-1}\frac{s}{2}[\Phi_0, K].
\end{equation}
\end{prop}


\begin{cor}[The Noether integrals] Let $u(\ell)_K=\{\xi\in u(\ell)|[\xi,K]=0\}$ be the isotropy subalgebra of $K$ within $u(\ell)$.
Then $\pr_{u(\ell)_K}\Phi_s$ is a first integral of the equations of motion, where the projection is considered with respect to an $\Ad$-invariant scalar product
on $u(\ell)$.
\end{cor}


\begin{rem}\label{remark1}
In particular,  since $u(\ell)_K$ contains all diagonal matrices in $u(\ell)$, from the diagonal components $(\Phi_s)_{i,i}$, we get the first integrals of motion $\Phi_{2i, 2i-1}$ given by \eqref{eq:fiovi} in the original coordinates.
If $\kappa_{2i-1,2i}=\kappa_{2j-1,2j}$, then we get that the $(i,j)$-th component of $\Phi_s$  is a first integral  of motion.
We have
\begin{equation}\label{eq:psiovi}
(\Phi_s)_{i,j}=\frac12(w_i\bar z_j-z_i\bar w_j)+s\sqrt{-1}\frac{\kappa_{2i-1,2i}}{4}(z_i\bar z_j+\bar z_i z_j),
\end{equation}
where the imaginary and real parts of $(\Phi_s)_{i,j}$ provide first integrals, which, multiplied by $-1/2$, coincide with  
the first integrals $\Psi^1_{2i-1,2i;2j-1,2j}$ and $\Psi^2_{2i-1,2i;2j-1,2j}$ obtained in \cite{DGJ2025}:
$
\Psi_{2i-1, 2i; 2j-1,2j}^1=(\gamma_{2i}p_{2j-1}-\gamma_{2j-1}p_{2i})-(\gamma_{2i-1}p_{2j}-\gamma_{2j}p_{2i-1})
-s\kappa_{2i-1,2i}(\gamma_{2i-1}\gamma_{2j-1}+\gamma_{2i}\gamma_{2j})$ and 
$\Psi_{2i-1, 2i; 2j-1,2j}^2=(\gamma_{2i-1}p_{2j-1}-\gamma_{2j-1}p_{2i-1})+(\gamma_{2i}p_{2j}-\gamma_{2j}p_{2i})
-s\kappa_{2i-1,2i}(\gamma_{2i-1}\gamma_{2j}-\gamma_{2i}\gamma_{2j-1})$.
\end{rem}

By a direct calculations we get the following statement.

\begin{prop}\label{pr:zzbar} The time derivative of $z\otimes \bar z$ can be expressed as follows:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:f01}
({z\otimes \bar z})^{\cdot}=2[\Phi_0, z\otimes \bar z].
\end{equation}
\end{prop}

 From Proposition \ref{pr:phis} and Proposition \ref{pr:zzbar}, we prove the following theorem.

\begin{thm} The equations  \eqref{eq:f0} and \eqref{eq:f01} imply
$$
\begin{aligned}
(z\otimes \bar z)^\cdot&=2[\Phi_s, z\otimes \bar z]+\frac{\sqrt{-1}s}{2}[z\otimes\bar z,K],\\
\dot \Phi_s&=\sqrt{-1}\frac{s}{2}[\Phi_0, K]+\frac{s^2}{8}[z\otimes \bar z,K^2].
\end{aligned}
$$
\end{thm}

Using the previous theorem, we construct a Lax representation  with a spectral parameter of the equations of motion \eqref{com1}:

\begin{thm}\label{th:LA} Consider the matrices
\begin{equation}\label{eq:LA}
L(\lambda)=-\lambda^2\frac{s}{16}K^2+\lambda \Phi_s +z\otimes \bar z; \quad A(\lambda)=\sqrt{-1}\frac{s}{2}K+\lambda^{-1}2z\otimes \bar z,
\end{equation}
where
$$
K=\diag(\kappa_{1,2}, \kappa_{3,4}, \dots, \kappa_{2\ell-1, 2\ell})
$$
and
$$
\Phi_s=\frac{1}{2}(\omega\otimes \bar z - z\otimes \bar \omega)+\sqrt{-1}\frac{s}{4}(Kz\otimes \bar z +z\otimes \bar zK).
$$
The equations of motion \eqref{com1} imply the Lax representation
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Lax}
\dot L(\lambda)=[L(\lambda), A(\lambda)].
\end{equation}

\end{thm}

The matrix $L(\lambda)$ in \eqref{eq:LA} is analogous to the Lax matrix for the Neumann system  from \cite{M1}. Thus, as in the Neumann case, starting from the matrix $L(\lambda)$ in \eqref{eq:LA}, we get, in addition to $\pr_{u(\ell)_K}\Phi_s$, quadratic first integrals that Poisson commute between themselves and also
commute with the Noether first integrals.
In the case of an even-dimensional sphere $S^{2\ell-2}$, when $n=2\ell-1$, we set $\kappa_{2\ell-1,2\ell}=0$ and note that the manifold
$\{\gamma_{2l}=0,p_{2l}=0\}$ is invariant under the flow of \eqref{com1}, and we get immediately a set of first integrals in this case as well.





\begin{thm}\label{th:intalln}
Assume that all $\kappa_{2i-1, 2i}$ are distinct.
The magnetic  flows \eqref{com1} are Liouville integrable on $T^*S^n$ for all $n$ by means of the linear Noether integrals $\Phi_{2i-1,2i}$ and the quadratic first integrals obtained from the Lax representation.
\end{thm}

In the case when some of $\kappa_{2i-1, 2i}$ are equal,
by adding all the Noether first integrals of the type \eqref{eq:psiovi}, we get non-commutative integrability.













