\textbf{3.10.7 Solving the general trinomial equation with periodic radicals} \label{chapter:chapter_X}

In (1758) Lambert considered the trinomial equation $x^m + q - x = 0$ which he solved with a series in terms of $q$. Subsequently resolved the form $a \cdot q \cdot x^p + x^q - 1 = 0$ which according to the definition $^d\sqrt{x} = x^{1/d}, d \in \mathbb{Q}_{+} - \{0\}$ can be given in nested radicals:

\[
 x = \sqrt[q]{1 - \alpha \cdot q \sqrt[q/p]{1 - \alpha \cdot q \sqrt[q/p]{1 - \cdots}}}
\]

\textbf{3.10.8 Solving the general form}
